.SH MACROS
.\" We do _not_ mention ANSI X3.64 here.  It was initially issued in
.\" (or at least dated) 1979, and reputedly, was never revised or
.\" updated prior its withdrawal in 1994.  ANSI X3.64 did not address
.\" color features.
.\" See <https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/FIPS/fipspub86.pdf>,
.\" p. H-1 (PDF p. 81).
ISO\ 6429 and ECMA-48
define eight standard colors
(known inaccurately as \*(``ANSI\*('' colors),
to which X/Open Curses assigns symbolic names as object-like macros
.BR \%COLOR_BLACK ","
.BR \%COLOR_RED ","
.BR \%COLOR_GREEN ","
.BR \%COLOR_YELLOW ","
.BR \%COLOR_BLUE ","
.BR \%COLOR_MAGENTA ","
.BR \%COLOR_CYAN ","
and
.BR \%COLOR_WHITE "."
.I curses
assumes that
.B \%COLOR_BLACK
is the default background color for all terminals.
.I \%ncurses
offers an extension to override that assumption;
see \fB\%assume_default_colors\fP(3X).
Some terminals support additional colors that lack standard names.
.PP
.B \%A_COLOR
is a bit mask that,
when bitwise \*(``and\*(''-ed with a
.IR \%chtype ","
extracts its color pair identifier.
X/Open Curses mandates the provision of two function-like macros.
They have no application in the wide-character API of
.IR curses "."
.SS COLOR_PAIR
.BI \%COLOR_PAIR( n )
replaces color pair identifier
.I n
with its encoded value appropriate for use in a
.IR chtype "."
Such values have a limited,
implementation-dependent range.
Non-wide API functions such as \fB\%attrset\fP(3X)
cannot handle larger color pair identifiers than this.
A portable application checks that
.IR n 's
value is less than
.I COLOR_PAIRS
before employing this macro on it.
If you need to transform a larger color pair identifier,
you must use the wide API and call,
for example,
\fB\%attr_set\fP(3X),
which passes the color pair identifier
as a parameter separate from the attributes.
.SS PAIR_NUMBER
.BI \%PAIR_NUMBER( n )
replaces its
.I chtype
or
.I attr_t
argument
.I n
with the color pair identifier encoded within it.
.\" X/Open Curses Issue 7 explains that terribly; see p. 57 (PDF p. 71).
.PP
.B \%COLOR_PAIR()
and
.B \%PAIR_NUMBER()
are inverse operations.
